52d03"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-106934" :question/id #uuid "0759ede7-6062-5a98-ab6f-920712fd67b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-106934" :question/problem "In a restaurant kitchen, a pot of soup reaches a temperature of ${200}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning off the stove, the temperature $T(t)$ of the soup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the kitchen temperature is ${76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the pot temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={76}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-76).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-106934/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-106934"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection/cd3647f13cdd93f054c3eb2962b71839a156fc1d9bcf1783b918f009045834df" :answer/id #uuid "ef570b0b-4090-5e12-a6ba-fbc3677ffc3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection/83521de023057e02b98cd57129aff2b19f28e1c5a6f0ec53211e083e7ef0d227" :answer/id #uuid "409ba1ce-74ab-529c-bfb5-77536e76250e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection/bc9cff6f1f25765138a176f241dc3b5705c7772a0b2f01ea8c769d8eed753d0f" :answer/id #uuid "178fd248-dc39-513e-aaec-c1e0517f04f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection/90329396138dd76fa21a1953e5f1b89ef0e13d51e2cd2fa5527fef40bfc6fd96" :answer/id #uuid "d5a03b42-d1df-5bf4-8b47-350715fdaf9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection/abf5ac6f554616c1b50f032a7005f35fd04497f3e4817d5a7877f2458a87da75" :answer/id #uuid "a7b8f96d-9a1f-5c51-8110-e9943a24685c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7443f0d6-6378-52ac-8682-db6bd720f948" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107125/selection/bc9cff6f1f25765138a176f241dc3b5705c7772a0b2f01ea8c769d8eed753d0f" :answer-field/choices ["q-107125/selection/cd3647f13cdd93f054c3eb2962b71839a156fc1d9bcf1783b918f009045834df" "q-107125/selection/83521de023057e02b98cd57129aff2b19f28e1c5a6f0ec53211e083e7ef0d227" "q-107125/selection/bc9cff6f1f25765138a176f241dc3b5705c7772a0b2f01ea8c769d8eed753d0f" "q-107125/selection/90329396138dd76fa21a1953e5f1b89ef0e13d51e2cd2fa5527fef40bfc6fd96" "q-107125/selection/abf5ac6f554616c1b50f032a7005f35fd04497f3e4817d5a7877f2458a87da75"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107125" :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-107125/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107125"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection/e06208f1a16efc864a247444ec3a97130690b7eb59cac7a42b7a8982f21d1b74" :answer/id #uuid "81fb3f4d-5f1f-535b-a29d-b61b1391cb75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection/76e0b5d59daeb310a731b9257683af76189b94a5cb6a4a30d166403525969a76" :answer/id #uuid "673f9fdd-d45e-5e35-9a8f-8fadfbef6844" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection/5a47e0d31864465600ddf6e881922a69b1c41cda283d9e9e9de39d7aee3d072e" :answer/id #uuid "7371e460-d8f2-57cb-a9d9-2aca752430dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection/92cfaaa71da699ab6a3ee911c03bb41e0bfbf70d90ce806749c5dd717aa91f50" :answer/id #uuid "6e67afe9-ad00-5c70-ab76-7a59e39a896b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection/422da04d352369db6a308755bcced7346930a422e11981321eb260295f3831a1" :answer/id #uuid "52cdde1c-82d5-5b64-b35a-706e91dbd8e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a791edc1-78b5-56ba-b67e-872ee0d0c530" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107120/selection/76e0b5d59daeb310a731b9257683af76189b94a5cb6a4a30d166403525969a76" :answer-field/choices ["q-107120/selection/e06208f1a16efc864a247444ec3a97130690b7eb59cac7a42b7a8982f21d1b74" "q-107120/selection/76e0b5d59daeb310a731b9257683af76189b94a5cb6a4a30d166403525969a76" "q-107120/selection/5a47e0d31864465600ddf6e881922a69b1c41cda283d9e9e9de39d7aee3d072e" "q-107120/selection/92cfaaa71da699ab6a3ee911c03bb41e0bfbf70d90ce806749c5dd717aa91f50" "q-107120/selection/422da04d352369db6a308755bcced7346930a422e11981321eb260295f3831a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107120" :question/id #uuid "59bdbd4d-7a55-5d08-9c2c-8e53aca29431" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-107120/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107120"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection/78370e4263cc27a90f67891f92375a8bf28ca812f1bd76811b00da3570fc962e" :answer/id #uuid "f549df01-be4a-5b50-a029-fa0f1c416859" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "95\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection/81a7d6f4b2bdc9b8e9cec4b954ca4669e7ce894d2451c91ca777f780936c384e" :answer/id #uuid "b7c22ba1-ff89-52e4-8388-78ef1fe4e335" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "98\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection/b34caf75d45c2434d6db648f7d4eb2cc3aa66126e4c7fa3f14b308a6265ea097" :answer/id #uuid "ee4eac05-e674-55f3-8197-e2cbcb3b7367" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "89\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection/e385ce8df70f14ca2e692d3af7a0b0a16669f00373850da3011cac836bd689df" :answer/id #uuid "be41c75a-ea0e-55c3-b887-8e93e3737fea" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "67\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection/2cf3041fe613092a3cb91167de3811d7c800363142e441d7816e6898b8c506a7" :answer/id #uuid "f39fce48-2643-5117-bdc9-074edebbb4d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "87\\,\\text{min}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "5a0c446b-2ba8-5781-b3f2-0c64025c5166" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-15589/selection/78370e4263cc27a90f67891f92375a8bf28ca812f1bd76811b00da3570fc962e" :answer-field/choices ["q-15589/selection/78370e4263cc27a90f67891f92375a8bf28ca812f1bd76811b00da3570fc962e" "q-15589/selection/81a7d6f4b2bdc9b8e9cec4b954ca4669e7ce894d2451c91ca777f780936c384e" "q-15589/selection/b34caf75d45c2434d6db648f7d4eb2cc3aa66126e4c7fa3f14b308a6265ea097" "q-15589/selection/e385ce8df70f14ca2e692d3af7a0b0a16669f00373850da3011cac836bd689df" "q-15589/selection/2cf3041fe613092a3cb91167de3811d7c800363142e441d7816e6898b8c506a7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-15589" :question/id #uuid "02ce9872-8986-5e67-bea1-b49d589c24ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-15589" :question/problem "The water boils in a pan at a temperature of ${100}^{∘}C.$ The pan with water is then placed to a room with a temperature of ${25}^{∘}C,$ and the temperature of water decreases. After $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the water to have a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the water in the pan decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the water starts cooling.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={25}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, it cools to ${90}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=90,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}90 & =25+75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 65 & =75{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{13}{15} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the water has a temperature of ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 25+75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 75{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =5 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the temperature of water will be ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{15})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{13}{15})}≈95\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-15589/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-15589"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection/b0f211da407228986048797785c7e328588aa59259a2f03e6f9d05d60eb4b1ef" :answer/id #uuid "ac834029-7b61-5c03-a6e6-7240c3cc3cef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection/1d65db27f6240b5947974c2b87b03346e7913eabaf393a124f3859b95d2cbb40" :answer/id #uuid "327b36d3-819c-5438-8676-c37d16c08a13" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{96}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection/72c26d9a4fed76768b9dc58e042080a561c28722547403b395cc8f3928fa10b9" :answer/id #uuid "e40cf18d-65d9-52e2-8fbc-73e0e1d53b21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection/7fe05b2d521bc0ddd9a76d57399ecb451c30916bc4190a30ef4d8bcc55cff744" :answer/id #uuid "5edb4d4e-1d84-5cd0-bd44-852653396e18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection/24bce9d93ac1f05c8b8938bca6949c3c5d90c6cf9cdd820b1d0f5a5dc271cc6f" :answer/id #uuid "d26ecd7d-434d-58ba-932e-05a9fcbcd237" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{76}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc2435ba-70d9-51e5-b442-284e42d013fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107118/selection/1d65db27f6240b5947974c2b87b03346e7913eabaf393a124f3859b95d2cbb40" :answer-field/choices ["q-107118/selection/b0f211da407228986048797785c7e328588aa59259a2f03e6f9d05d60eb4b1ef" "q-107118/selection/1d65db27f6240b5947974c2b87b03346e7913eabaf393a124f3859b95d2cbb40" "q-107118/selection/72c26d9a4fed76768b9dc58e042080a561c28722547403b395cc8f3928fa10b9" "q-107118/selection/7fe05b2d521bc0ddd9a76d57399ecb451c30916bc4190a30ef4d8bcc55cff744" "q-107118/selection/24bce9d93ac1f05c8b8938bca6949c3c5d90c6cf9cdd820b1d0f5a5dc271cc6f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107118" :question/id #uuid "6b4e1aaa-5dbc-5088-a3aa-1be98695474d" :question/math-academy-id "q-107118" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cooling iron can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the iron is ${140}^{∘}C,$ calculate its temperature after $5$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cooling iron is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.1(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-0.1\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-\\int 0.1\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-0.1t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-0.1t+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-0.1t} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-0.1t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the iron was ${140}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =28+K \\\\ K & =112\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+112{e}^{-0.1t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the iron after $t=5$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(5)=28+112{e}^{-0.1\\cdot 5}≈{96}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-107118/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107118"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection/1f41efacd63154abf9d2972c6606a44bf4a632574e4662cf53230da141cb8538" :answer/id #uuid "1aa5f8e7-ec62-5131-9299-59a9b45fea82" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{31}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection/b0ff619af33876b64425598cde099488043fa22566dfea426a45dc28599c7363" :answer/id #uuid "f6bd7f09-58df-5306-8c07-0d976c872560" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{29}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection/7b9cfb45ac9e3cf4478f5c0a39ca353a354683e52cda278cda40fa1a351abde1" :answer/id #uuid "d48b804c-44be-5140-bb6f-e1fdac43fdf3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{58}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection/a4d8b4de478e2060e9fc24be9097299605f4764926e9c3554d8c1d903179d620" :answer/id #uuid "a0121b34-eb76-52da-ba6d-2b1b3c087d09" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{38}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection/207427a4f6f31160d7defbe1b38504469143245e3b8b9e063c8a432386ce07d3" :answer/id #uuid "a9bb5d32-72cd-58e5-879d-f1cc274c62fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{43}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "2b546eac-0f98-51fa-8f14-4ed027e28fc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-107129/selection/a4d8b4de478e2060e9fc24be9097299605f4764926e9c3554d8c1d903179d620" :answer-field/choices ["q-107129/selection/1f41efacd63154abf9d2972c6606a44bf4a632574e4662cf53230da141cb8538" "q-107129/selection/b0ff619af33876b64425598cde099488043fa22566dfea426a45dc28599c7363" "q-107129/selection/7b9cfb45ac9e3cf4478f5c0a39ca353a354683e52cda278cda40fa1a351abde1" "q-107129/selection/a4d8b4de478e2060e9fc24be9097299605f4764926e9c3554d8c1d903179d620" "q-107129/selection/207427a4f6f31160d7defbe1b38504469143245e3b8b9e063c8a432386ce07d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-107129" :question/id #uuid "166a478b-a216-5747-8d39-a0615baddbf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-107129" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a compressor of a refrigerator can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the compressor is ${65}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the compressor after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the compressor of a refrigerator is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.12(T-21).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-0.12\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-21} & =-\\int 0.12\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-21| & =-0.12t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-21>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-21 & ={e}^{-0.12t+C} \\\\ T-21 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T-21 & =K{e}^{-0.12t} \\\\ T & =21+K{e}^{-0.12t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the compressor was ${65}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=65,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}65 & =21+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 65 & =21+K \\\\ K & =44\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=21+44{e}^{-0.12t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the compressor after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=21+44{e}^{-0.12\\cdot 8}≈{38}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-107129/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-107129"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8957"] :question/problem "Given that $y > 50$ and $t > 0,$ which of the following is an inhibited decay equation?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600+12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y \\[5pt]$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y^2$" :question/worked-solution "An inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -k(y - a),\n$$\n\nwhere $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $y > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nFrom the given options, II is the only inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} =600-12y.\n$$\n\nTo see this, let's factor the right-hand side of the equation. This gives,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = -12(y-50).\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=12,$ and $a=50.$ Note that for inhibited decay, we must have $y > 50$ for all $t > 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6676"] :question/problem "A loaf of bread just taken out of the oven has a temperature of $180^{\\circ}\\textrm{C}.$ After placing it on the table, the temperature $T(t)$ of the bread decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, and $t > 0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is $25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the bread's temperature? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, $T_S = 25^{\\circ}\\textrm{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d T}{\\textrm d t} = -k(T - 25).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-6677"] :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a fluorescent light bulb after turning off can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23)\n$$\n\nwhere $t > 0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the bulb is $80^\\circ \\rm C,$ calculate the temperature of the bulb after $4$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fluorescent light bulb is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}T}{\\mathrm{d}t} =  -0.25(T-23).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-0.25\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-23} & =-\\int 0.25\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-23| & =-0.25t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T - 23 > 0$ for all $t > 0.$ \nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-23 & ={e}^{-0.25t+C} \\\\ T-23 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T-23 & =K{e}^{-0.25t} \\\\ T & =23+K{e}^{-0.25t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the light bulb was $80^\\circ \\rm C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=80,$ and solve for $K{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}80 & =23+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 80 & =23+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t) = 23+57e^{-0.25t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the bulb after $t=4$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(4) = 23+57e^{-0.25\\cdot 4}\\approx 44^\\circ\\textrm{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer." :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
